Current Architecture Issues: Experience API / Core API Directly Calling Guidewire
1. Tight Coupling to Guidewire
Experience APIs and Core APIs are directly coupled to Guidewire APIs and data models.
Changes to Guidewire APIs, schemas, or business processes require changes across multiple consuming applications.
Guidewire-specific concepts are leaking into channel-facing APIs, reducing flexibility.
Difficult to replace, upgrade, or introduce additional policy platforms in the future.
2. Lack of Domain Boundary and Business Capability Layer
Business capabilities are not clearly separated from system integration concerns.
APIs are orchestrating Guidewire transactions instead of invoking business capabilities.
Business logic may become duplicated across Experience APIs and Core APIs.
No single ownership point for business capabilities such as Policy, Driver, Vehicle, Quote, and Rating.
3. Resiliency Challenges (Critical)
Single Point of Failure Dependency
Experience APIs and Core APIs have a direct dependency on Guidewire availability.
Any Guidewire outage immediately impacts all consuming applications.
No abstraction layer exists to provide alternate service behavior during downstream failures.
Lack of Graceful Degradation
Applications cannot easily switch to read-only capability during Guidewire outages.
No mechanism exists to serve customer requests from read replicas or cached data.
Customers experience complete service failure instead of limited functionality.

Example:

Current:

Customer
   |
Experience API
   |
Guidewire DOWN
   |
Customer Request Failed

Target:

Customer
   |
Experience API
   |
Capability Layer
   |
Guidewire DOWN
   |
Read Replica / Alternate Source
   |
Read-only Experience
4. Insufficient Failure Isolation
A Guidewire performance issue can cascade to multiple upstream applications.
Long-running Guidewire requests can consume API resources.
No centralized circuit breaker strategy exists.
No consistent timeout policies across consumers.

Potential impacts:

Thread exhaustion
Connection pool exhaustion
Increased response times
Cascading failures
5. Inconsistent Resiliency Patterns
Each API implements its own approach for:
Timeout handling
Retry logic
Error handling
Circuit breakers
Fallback behavior
No enterprise resiliency standards are enforced.
Different teams may implement conflicting recovery strategies.
6. No Centralized Integration Governance
Multiple applications directly manage Guidewire integrations.
Authentication, token management, logging, and monitoring are duplicated.
Guidewire API consumption patterns are inconsistent.
Difficult to enforce enterprise standards.
7. Increased Operational Complexity
Production troubleshooting requires understanding multiple direct integrations.
No centralized view of Guidewire dependency health.
Difficult to determine which applications are impacted during Guidewire incidents.
Incident resolution requires coordination across multiple teams.
8. Poor Observability
End-to-end transaction tracing is difficult.
Customer journey visibility is limited.
Difficult to identify whether failures originate from:
Experience API
Core API
Guidewire
Network
Database

Missing capabilities:

Distributed tracing
Dependency monitoring
Business transaction monitoring
SLA tracking
9. Scalability Concerns
Increasing customer traffic directly increases load on Guidewire.
No capability layer to aggregate, optimize, or cache requests.
Duplicate requests may hit Guidewire from multiple consumers.
Difficult to introduce caching strategies.
10. Duplicate Integration Logic

Multiple applications may implement:

Guidewire authentication
Request transformation
Response mapping
Error handling
Retry logic
API version handling

This creates:

Code duplication
Higher maintenance cost
Inconsistent behavior
11. Difficulty Supporting Multiple Systems of Record

Future state may require:

Guidewire PolicyCenter
Legacy Mainframe Policy System
Guidewire Read Replica
Future policy platform

Current architecture makes this difficult because consumers directly know the source system.

Example:

Current:

Experience API
      |
      |
Guidewire

Future requirement:

Policy Capability
      |
      |
Policy Gateway
      |
      +---- Guidewire
      |
      +---- Mainframe
      |
      +---- Read Replica
12. Limited Support for AI and New Channels
AI assistants cannot easily reuse existing business capabilities.
New channels require direct knowledge of Guidewire APIs.
AI integrations may create another set of point-to-point integrations.
Business capabilities are not exposed as reusable enterprise services.
13. Difficulty Implementing Enterprise Process Orchestration
End-to-end journeys are distributed across Experience APIs.
Long-running processes are difficult to manage.
No centralized handling for:
Workflow state
Compensation
Human approval
Exception management
14. Security Concerns
Multiple applications maintain direct Guidewire access.
Larger attack surface.
Difficult to enforce consistent:
Authorization policies
API throttling
Audit requirements
Data masking
15. Testing Complexity
Every consumer requires Guidewire integration testing.
Difficult to test applications independently.
Requires Guidewire availability for many application test cycles.
Limited ability to mock or virtualize downstream dependencies.
Executive Summary Slide (Short Version)

If you need a single slide, I would summarize:

Current Architecture Challenges

Direct coupling between Experience/Core APIs and Guidewire.
Lack of business capability abstraction.
Guidewire availability becomes a customer-facing dependency.
Limited resiliency patterns (timeouts, circuit breakers, fallback).
No graceful degradation during downstream outages.
Duplicate integration logic across applications.
Difficult to support multiple systems of record.
Limited reuse for AI, mobile, and future channels.
Poor end-to-end observability and operational visibility.
Increased cost and complexity of future modernization.

